 21%|##        | 125/600 [04:03<10:44,  1.36s/it][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nAn expert made statistics on the life expectancy of the late Nobel laureates in economics and found that their average life expectancy was 85 years old, of which most were over 90 years old, many were over 100 years old, and the youngest died at the age of 74 years. As a result, the expert believes that winning the Nobel Prize in economics can make people live longer.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, would most weaken the opinions of the above-mentioned experts?\n# Options:\nA) The nobel prize in economics is only awarded to living scholars. this awarding rule is extremely beneficial to scholars who have lived a long life\nB) Winning the nobel prize can bring great physical and mental pleasure to fame, and a happy physical and mental state can prolong life\nC) Keynes, the father of macroeconomics, passed away at the age of 63. it is a pity that he did not win the nobel prize in economics\nD) Scholars who have won the nobel prize in physics have a long life span, but none of them have won the nobel prize in economics', "<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option most effectively weakens the expert's claim that winning the Nobel Prize in Economics can lead to a longer life expectancy.Let's analyze each option step by step:.\n\nA) **The nobel prize in economics is only awarded to living scholars.this awarding rule is extremely beneficial to scholars who have lived a long life**.\n   - This option supports the expert's claim by suggesting that the prize is given to scholars who have already lived a long time, thus reinforcing the idea that winning the prize might contribute to longevity.\n\nB) **Winning the nobel prize can bring great physical and mental pleasure to fame, and a happy physical and mental state can prolong life**.\n   - This option actually supports the expert's claim by suggesting that winning the prize can lead to a longer life through positive mental and physical effects.\n\nC) **Keynes, the father of macroeconomics, passed away at the age of 63. it is fortunate that he did not win the nobel prize in economics**.\n   - This option provides a counterexample by mentioning that a well-known economist (Keynes) did not win the prize and still had a relatively short life. This directly challenges the expert's claim that winning the prize leads to longevity.\n\nD) **Scholars who have won the nobel prize in physics have a long life span, but none of them have won the nobel prize in economics**.\n   - This option does not provide any direct evidence to weaken the expert's claim. It merely states that there is no correlation between winning the Nobel Prize in Physics and longevity, but it does not address the specific claim about economics\n. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
