messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is closest to the meaning of the above judgement?\n# Options:\nA) If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.\nB) Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\nC) If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\nD) To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.', '<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given context and analyze each option step by step.\n\n1.**Context Analysis:**.\n   - The context states: "If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.".\n   - This implies a conditional relationship where closing all wrong doors leads to the shutting out of truth.\n\n2.**Understanding the Conditional Statement:**.\n   - The statement can be rephrased as: "Closing all wrong doors → Truth is shut out.".\n   - This is a classic form of a conditional statement: If P, then Q (P → Q).\n\n3.**Analyzing Each Option:**.\n   - **Option A:** "If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.".\n     - This is the contrapositive of the original statement.The contrapositive of P → Q is ¬Q → ¬P.\n     - The contrapositive of "Closing all wrong doors → Truth is shut out" is "Truth is not shut out → You do not close all the wrong doors.".\n     - This is not the same as the original statement, so Option A is incorrect.\n\n   - **Option B:** "Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.".\n- This could be misunderstood as: "Lies are welcome and the right doors should be open.".\n     - This is the inverse of the original claim, which we have already confirmed is illogically equivalent.\n     - Hence, Option A is the correct choice.\n\n   - **Option B:** "When lies are embraced, the doors to the right should remain closed.".\n     - This differs slightly from Option A, mentioning "doors to the right" instead of "right doors."\n     - Despite this variation, the essence remains the same as Option A, making Option B the correct selection.\n\n   - **Option C:** "By rejecting the truth, we can accept what is wrong.".\n     - This response is not directly applicable to the provided scenario\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
